.TH AVR\-VM\-SSH\-CONFIG 1 "" "avr 0.1.6" "Avrea CLI"
.SH NAME
avr-vm-ssh-config \- Print (or --append) an ssh_config Host block for a RUNNING VM.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B avr vm ssh-config
[OPTIONS] VM_ID
.SH DESCRIPTION
Print (or --append) an ssh_config Host block for a RUNNING VM.
.PP
Reach the VM with plain `ssh`, scp/rsync, and VS Code / Cursor Remote-SSH, host key pinned, without wrapping each tool. Redirect it yourself:
.PP
avr vm ssh-config cvm-abc123 >> ~/.ssh/config
.PP
or let --append manage the block for you (idempotent — re-run after a restart to refresh the endpoint in place):
.PP
avr vm ssh-config cvm-abc123 --append
.PP
The block references a dedicated known_hosts file that this command writes the pinned host key into (one entry per VM). If the endpoint publishes no host key, the block falls back to accept-new (trust-on-first-use) and a warning is printed.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
\fIVM_ID\fR
\&
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-org\fR \fITEXT\fR
Organization ID. Uses default org if not specified (see: avr config set org).
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-identity\fR \fIPATH\fR
IdentityFile to write into the block.
.TP
\fB\-\-host\-alias\fR \fITEXT\fR
Host alias for the block (default: avr-<vm-id>).
.TP
\fB\-\-known\-hosts\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
Where to pin the host key (default: ~/.ssh/avr_known_hosts).
.TP
\fB\-\-append\fR
Write the block into your SSH config (default: ~/.ssh/config) instead of printing it, replacing any prior block for the same alias in place.
.TP
\fB\-\-config\-file\fR \fIPATH\fR
SSH config file for --append (default: ~/.ssh/config).
